<div class="page-break"></div>

<!-- ======================================================= -->
<!-- PAGE 1: IMAGE 1, PROLOGUE (1965) & ACT I (1942)         -->
<!-- ======================================================= -->
<div class="page">

  <!-- IMAGE PLACEHOLDER 1: BEFORE FADE IN -->
  <div class="photo-frame">
    <div class="photo-placeholder">
      [ PHOTO INSERT: LINA HEYDRICH &bull; CIRCA 1938 &bull; MONOCHROME ]
    </div>
    <div class="photo-caption">Fig. 1.0 — Lina Heydrich (née von Osten), Baltic stock, Fehmarn.</div>
  </div>

  <div class="transition">FADE IN:</div>

  <div class="slugline">INT. CZECHOSLOVAK CULTURAL CENTER, EAST BERLIN - AUDITORIUM - NIGHT (AUTUMN 1965)</div>
  <p class="score-note">[SCORE: Silence, save for the rhythmic, low CHATTER of a 35mm projector.]</p>
  <p class="action">A stark cone of projector light cuts through cigarette haze toward a canvas screen.</p>
  <p class="action">ON SCREEN (IN 35MM BLACK-AND-WHITE): The 1964 film <em>Atentát</em>. A low camera tracks an open dark Mercedes-Benz slowing around the hairpin curve in Prague-Libeň. Two men in trench coats step from the curb.</p>
  <p class="action">IN THE AUDITORIUM: Sitting isolated in row six is LINA HEYDRICH (54). Severe wool coat, hair drawn back into a plain knot. The flickering silver light sharpens her jawline.</p>
  <p class="action">ON SCREEN: The paratrooper raises a Sten gun. Pulls the trigger. CLICK. The bolt jams.</p>
  <p class="action">IN THE AUDITORIUM: Lina does not flinch. Her gloved fingers rise to her throat. She touches a strand of dull FRESHWATER PEARLS resting above her collar, rolling the center pearl against her collarbone.</p>
  <p class="action">ON SCREEN: The second paratrooper hurls an anti-tank grenade. It shatters against the rear wheel housing. A DEAFENING BLAST.</p>
  <p class="score-note">[SCORE: The explosion cuts instantly into a single, high, vibrating violin tone: J.S. BACH — PARTITA NO. 2 IN D MINOR, BWV 1004 (CHACONNE). Solo violin, austere and piercing.]</p>

  <div class="transition">DISSOLVE TO:</div>

  <div class="slugline">EXT. CASTLE JUNGFERN-BRESCHAN - COURTYARD - MORNING (MAY 27, 1942)</div>
  <p class="action">Blinding May sunlight. High lilacs in violet bloom. An open dark-green Mercedes 320 idles on the stones.</p>
  <p class="action">LINA (30), heavily pregnant, stands on the terrace. Her apron bears faint traces of garden loam. Beside her, KLAUS (8) and HEIDER (7) chase each other across the grass.</p>
  <p class="action">Lina catches Klaus by the wrist, gently wiping a streak of soil from his temple with her hem.</p>

  <div class="character">LINA</div>
  <div class="dialogue">Stay clear of the main gate, Klauschen. The trucks have blind corners.</div>

  <div class="character">KLAUS</div>
  <div class="dialogue">Father promised to show me the black mare when he returns!</div>

</div>

<div class="page-break"></div>

<!-- ======================================================= -->
<!-- PAGE 2: DEPARTURE, BULOVKA & BLANK RESEARCH SECTION     -->
<!-- ======================================================= -->
<div class="page">

  <p class="action">REINHARD HEYDRICH (38) steps from the double terrace doors. Field-grey tunic, cap tucked under his arm. His long, pale hand rests over Lina’s shoulder. His fingers lightly brush the pearls at her neck.</p>

  <div class="character">HEYDRICH</div>
  <div class="dialogue">Two days with the Führer in Berlin. Then straight home.</div>

  <div class="character">LINA</div>
  <div class="parenthetical">(hand lingering on his cuff)</div>
  <div class="dialogue">Take the closed staff car, Reinhard. Just this once.</div>

  <div class="character">HEYDRICH</div>
  <div class="parenthetical">(a quiet, thin smile)</div>
  <div class="dialogue">My Czechs know their master is not afraid. A roof invites ambush. The wind keeps the head clear.</div>

  <p class="action">He turns, touches Klaus’s blond hair, and climbs into the front seat beside his driver. The Mercedes turns down the gravel drive.</p>

  <div class="slugline">INT. BULOVKA HOSPITAL, PRAGUE - PRIVATE ROOM - DUSK (JUNE 2, 1942)</div>
  <p class="score-note">[SCORE: Bach’s Chaconne slows, dropping into a low, mournful drone.]</p>
  <p class="action">A sterile, shadowy room. Heydrich lies propped on white pillows, thick bandages swathing his torso. His cheeks are gray with fever sweat. Lina sits beside the bed, holding a bowl of clear broth carried from their kitchen.</p>

  <div class="character">LINA</div>
  <div class="dialogue">The fever will break tonight, Reinhard. The boys made drawings of the stable for you.</div>

  <p class="action">Heydrich’s dry lips move faintly.</p>

  <div class="character">HEYDRICH</div>
  <div class="dialogue">Denk an unsere Kinder... Take them to Fehmarn if the front collapses.</div>

  <div class="character">LINA</div>
  <div class="dialogue">Do not speak of collapses. You are the Protector.</div>

  <p class="action">His eyes stare upward into the dim plaster ceiling. His long fingers twitch on the bedsheet, tracing the silent fingerings of a violin passage.</p>

  <!-- BLANK USER-FILLABLE RESEARCH SECTION -->
  <div class="historical-box blank-section">
    <!-- USER ANNOTATION INSERTION POINT -->
    <strong>HISTORICAL NOTE:</strong> [SPACE RESERVED FOR RESEARCH ANNOTATION / ARCHIVAL RECORD / PERSONAL COMMENTARY]
  </div>

</div>

<div class="page-break"></div>

<!-- ======================================================= -->
<!-- PAGE 3: ACT II: THE PLANT (HALLE 1931)                  -->
<!-- ======================================================= -->
<div class="page">

  <div class="character">LINA (V.O.)</div>
  <div class="dialogue">Before the empire, before the black coats and the dossiers, there was only a cashiered sailor sitting in his mother’s drafty parlor with his pride cut to ribbons.</div>

  <div class="transition">DISSOLVE TO:</div>

  <div class="slugline">INT. HEYDRICH FAMILY APARTMENT, HALLE - NIGHT (APRIL 1931)</div>
  <p class="score-note">[SCORE: A solitary upright piano plays J.S. BACH — SARABANDE FROM PARTITA IN B MINOR, haltingly, from down the hall.]</p>
  <p class="action">Rain beats against the tall windowpanes. Worn velvet armchairs. The wallpaper shows pale patches where family portraits were sold during the hyperinflation.</p>
  <p class="action">REINHARD (27) sits at the table in an ill-fitting civilian suit. His naval buttons have been sheared off. He stares down at his empty palms.</p>
  <p class="action">LINA (19), sharp-eyed, pours boiling water into a stone teapot.</p>

  <div class="character">HEYDRICH</div>
  <div class="dialogue">Discharged for dishonor. The Admiral signed the paper without looking up. Ten years of service erased because an admiral’s daughter took offense. My father cannot walk past the conservatory without neighbors whispering.</div>

  <div class="character">LINA</div>
  <div class="dialogue">Your father lives in thirty years ago. We live now. Look at me, Reinhard.</div>

  <p class="action">She steps to him, taking his cold hands. Heydrich pulls a small, scuffed leather case from his vest. Snaps it open.</p>
  <p class="action">Inside rests a delicate strand of freshwater pearls.</p>

  <div class="character">HEYDRICH</div>
  <div class="dialogue">My mother’s father gave them to her when she sang at the Dresden Opera. When the inflation wiped out the school, they sold the silver forks. She hid these in the piano case. She told me to sell them to pay our rent in Kiel.</div>

  <p class="action">He lifts the necklace. The dull pearls catch the gas lamp's glow.</p>

  <div class="character">HEYDRICH (CONT'D)</div>
  <div class="dialogue">They stripped my rank. They made me walk through the naval gates like a vagrant. I swear to you, Lina... they will never take anything from us again.</div>

  <p class="action">His long fingers reach behind her neck, fastening the silver clasp. The cold pearls settle firmly against her skin.</p>

  <div class="character">LINA</div>
  <div class="parenthetical">(touching the clasp)</div>
  <div class="dialogue">Then go to Munich. Let this man Himmler see what you can build.</div>

</div>

<div class="page-break"></div>

<!-- ======================================================= -->
<!-- PAGE 4: ACT III: THE TWO VIOLINS (BERLIN 1938)           -->
<!-- ======================================================= -->
<div class="page">

  <div class="transition">MATCH CUT TO:</div>

  <div class="slugline">INT. BERLIN-SCHLACHTENSEE - MUSIC ROOM - EVENING (DECEMBER 1938)</div>
  <p class="score-note">[SCORE: DIEGETIC MUSIC — J.S. BACH: CONCERTO FOR TWO VIOLINS IN D MINOR, BWV 1043 (LARGO MA NON TANTO).] </p>
  <p class="action">A fire blazes in the brick hearth. Heydrich, now in the black SS uniform with silver collar runes, stands shoulder-to-shoulder with ERIKA CANARIS.</p>
  <p class="action">Their violin bows rise and descend in razor precision. Heydrich plays with closed eyes, his narrow profile lit by the orange hearth. On the sofa, ADMIRAL WILHELM CANARIS sips Mosel wine from crystal.</p>
  <p class="action">Across the carpet, Lina sits with KLAUS (4) and toddler HEIDER. She builds a wooden railway with the boys, shushing them with gentle strokes.</p>

  <div class="character">KLAUS</div>
  <div class="parenthetical">(whispering)</div>
  <div class="dialogue">Look, Mother. The train is going through the mountain.</div>

  <div class="character">LINA</div>
  <div class="parenthetical">(kissing his temple)</div>
  <div class="dialogue">Quietly, my heart. Listen to your father’s bow.</div>

  <p class="action">The music pauses on an exquisite, suspended cadence. Heydrich lowers the instrument, his eyes opening with detached clarity.</p>

  <div class="character">CANARIS</div>
  <div class="dialogue">If our ministries could find that same resolution, Reinhard, Europe might sleep past dawn.</div>

  <div class="character">HEYDRICH</div>
  <div class="dialogue">Dissonance is only permitted when it leads to a decisive conclusion, Admiral. Otherwise, it is simply noise.</div>

  <p class="action">Lina looks from her husband’s black tunic down to Klaus, who has fallen asleep across her lap. Her hand touches the pearls at her neck.</p>

  <div class="character">LINA (V.O.)</div>
  <div class="dialogue">He put every ounce of tenderness he had left into Bach. For the world, he saved the iron. I told myself the home was ours, and the iron was merely the wall keeping the wolves out.</div>

</div>

<div class="page-break"></div>

<!-- ======================================================= -->
<!-- PAGE 5: ACT IV: THE STATE FUNERAL & KLAUS'S DEATH       -->
<!-- ======================================================= -->
<div class="page">

  <div class="transition">DISSOLVE TO:</div>

  <div class="slugline">INT. MOSAIC HALL, NEW REICH CHANCELLERY, BERLIN - DAY (JUNE 9, 1942)</div>
  <p class="score-note">[SCORE: A massive organ blasts WAGNER'S FUNERAL MARCH, hollow and deafening, obliterating all intimacy.]</p>
  <p class="action">A towering cavern of red marble, polished granite, and giant vertical swastika standards. Wreaths of blood-red carnations stacked ten feet high.</p>
  <p class="action">LINA stands in front, heavily veiled in dense black crêpe, her pregnancy straining against the fabric. Her knuckles lock around the small hands of KLAUS (8) and HEIDER (7), both dressed in tailored black velvet sailor suits.</p>
  <p class="action">ADOLF HITLER approaches along the marble runner, his face set in theatrical lamentation. He stops before the boys.</p>
  <p class="action">Hitler extends his black-gloved hands. He cups Klaus’s jaw, tilting the eight-year-old’s face upward toward the bronze ceiling.</p>

  <div class="character">HITLER</div>
  <div class="dialogue">Your father had an iron heart. You must be iron for the Reich.</div>

  <p class="action">Klaus’s chin quivers under the leather glove. His eyes dart in sheer terror toward his mother.</p>
  <p class="action">Lina takes half a step forward—a fierce, protective maternal instinct. Hitler withdraws his glove, turning toward the velvet cushion before the casket.</p>

  <div class="character">LINA (V.O.)</div>
  <div class="dialogue">In that tomb of marble, I saw what they had done. Reinhard was no longer my husband. He was no longer the father who lifted Klaus onto the mare. He had been eaten by the State theater. They made my boys stand like soldiers while Hitler used our dead man to feed his furnace.</div>

  <div class="transition">DISSOLVE TO:</div>

  <div class="slugline">EXT. CASTLE JUNGFERN-BRESCHAN - ENTRANCE GATE - LATE AFTERNOON (MAY 1943)</div>
  <p class="action">ONE YEAR LATER. A child's bicycle lies crushed in the gravel, its front wheel bent sideways.</p>
  <p class="action">Lina falls to her knees on the road. She gathers KLAUS (9) into her lap. The boy’s blond hair is soaked in crimson; his eyes are fixed on the clouds.</p>

  <div class="character">LINA</div>
  <div class="parenthetical">(a raw, broken choke)</div>
  <div class="dialogue">Klaus... Klauschen... Mother is here. Look at Mother.</div>

  <p class="action">The Czech lorry driver stands by his fender, palms pressed to his eyes. SS sentries run forward with rifles raised.</p>

  <div class="character">LINA (CONT'D)</div>
  <div class="parenthetical">(screaming at the guards)</div>
  <div class="dialogue">DON’T TOUCH HIM! LEAVE US ALONE!</div>

</div>

<div class="page-break"></div>

<!-- ======================================================= -->
<!-- PAGE 6: ACT V: THE DREAM ON FEHMARN                     -->
<!-- ======================================================= -->
<div class="page">

  <p class="score-note">[SCORE: Silence falls instantly, total and dry. The only sound is the cold Baltic wind.]</p>

  <div class="character">LINA (V.O.)</div>
  <div class="dialogue">They said the paratroopers killed Reinhard. But Klaus... Klaus was taken by a lorry on a sunlit afternoon. And that was when I knew: no uniform, no castle, no empire could keep anything safe.</div>

  <div class="transition">DISSOLVE TO:</div>

  <div class="slugline">EXT. FEHMARN ISLAND - BALTIC SHORE - DUSK (1960)</div>
  <p class="score-note">[SCORE: J.S. BACH — SOLO VIOLIN PARTITA NO. 2 (CHACONNE) enters again, distant, carried on the sound of crashing waves.]</p>
  <p class="action">Grey dunes. Sea grass whipping in the autumn gale. The weathered guesthouse <em>Imbria Parc</em> stands solitary against the grey horizon.</p>
  <p class="action">LINA (49) walks along the water’s edge in heavy boots and a wind-whipped woolen coat. The white foam churns over dark rocks.</p>
  <p class="action">Her hand reaches into her collar, rolling the freshwater pearls between her bare fingers.</p>

  <div class="character">LINA (V.O.)</div>
  <div class="dialogue">Sometimes, when the salt wind rattles the windows, it seems to me that it was all an operatic hallucination. Did those twelve years in Berlin and Prague ever exist? The red marble, the grand pianos, the firing squads, the telephone ringing at dawn... It feels like a nightmare dreamt by someone else.</div>

  <p class="action">She looks out toward the grey sea, her jaw set like stone.</p>

  <div class="character">LINA (V.O.) (CONT'D)</div>
  <div class="dialogue">They come from the courts with their stamps and subpoenas. They want me to spit on his memory so they can forgive themselves. But if I say he was a monster, then Klaus’s laughter was a lie. The violin was a lie. These pearls around my neck are the only proof that any of it was real. He was a soldier who held the line assigned to him. The rest... was the storm.</div>

</div>

<div class="page-break"></div>

<!-- ======================================================= -->
<!-- PAGE 7: EPILOGUE (BERLIN 1965) & IMAGE 2                -->
<!-- ======================================================= -->
<div class="page">

  <div class="transition">CUT TO:</div>

  <div class="slugline">INT. CZECHOSLOVAK CULTURAL CENTER - SCREENING ROOM - NIGHT (1965)</div>
  <p class="action">Back in the darkened auditorium on Friedrichstraße.</p>
  <p class="action">ON SCREEN: The church crypt in Prague. Water from fire hoses pours through the narrow pavement grates. Muzzle flashes light up the stone vaults as the paratroopers make their final stand.</p>
  <p class="action">IN THE AUDITORIUM: Lina stands up. Her grey coat hangs straight. She does not watch the men die.</p>
  <p class="action">She walks up the carpeted aisle, her back to the flickering light, pushing through the heavy velvet curtain.</p>

  <div class="slugline">INT. FOYER - CONTINUOUS</div>
  <p class="action">A nervous young CZECH CULTURAL ATTACHÉ turns from the coat desk.</p>

  <div class="character">ATTACHÉ</div>
  <div class="dialogue">Frau Heydrich... the screening is not yet over.</div>

  <p class="action">Lina pauses at the glass exit doors. Her fingers adjust the top button of her coat, concealing the freshwater pearls beneath the wool lapel.</p>

  <div class="character">LINA</div>
  <div class="parenthetical">(her tone entirely level)</div>
  <div class="dialogue">I have seen enough. Inform your director... I have no objections to the film.</div>

  <div class="slugline">EXT. FRIEDRICHSTRASSE, EAST BERLIN - CONTINUOUS</div>
  <p class="action">Lina pushes into the cold autumn night. Wet asphalt reflects the neon lights of the divided city.</p>
  <p class="action">She turns up her collar and walks into the drizzle, solitary and erect, until her silhouette disappears into the mist.</p>
  <p class="score-note">[SCORE: The solo Bach Chaconne concludes on an unresolved, lingering low D.]</p>

  <div class="transition">FADE TO BLACK.</div>

  <div class="end-card">
    LINA HEYDRICH RETURNED TO FEHMARN, WHERE SHE OPERATED<br>
    AN INN UNTIL HER DEATH IN 1985.<br><br>
    SHE REFUSED TO RENOUNCE HER HUSBAND OR ADMIT GUILT,<br>
    VIEWING HER LIFE AS A TRAGEDY BORNE OF DUTY.
  </div>

